    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This argument directly aligns with John Stuart Mill's defense of free expression, which posits that suppressing any opinion, even a wrong one, is dangerous because the truth might be contained within the suppressed idea. When the state defines \"misinformation,\" it inherently assumes a privileged, objective authority to judge truth, thereby stifling the critical cross-examination necessary for ideas to be rigorously tested. This intervention risks replacing organic debate with enforced consensus, which is a hallmark of authoritarian control.",
